1
00:00:09,444 --> 00:00:11,868
Firewood burns, and there is ash.

2
00:00:11,948 --> 00:00:15,555
Does something pass from the wood
into the ash? Or does nothing?

3
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:18,988
I am John Tan, known as Thusness.

4
00:00:19,068 --> 00:00:22,946
These are my own notes, from my
own practice and contemplation.

5
00:00:23,756 --> 00:00:28,635
In Part 7, the shore and the boat
took away the seat outside the event,

6
00:00:28,715 --> 00:00:33,273
and the doer behind it. A third way
something could stand outside the event,

7
00:00:33,353 --> 00:00:35,365
and complete it, is time.

8
00:00:35,445 --> 00:00:37,254
A past that leads up to it.

9
00:00:37,334 --> 00:00:39,047
A future it leads into.

10
00:00:39,127 --> 00:00:41,755
A line, on which it is one small point.

11
00:00:42,552 --> 00:00:45,330
Dōgen's image for this
is firewood and ash.

12
00:00:45,410 --> 00:00:50,139
Of his four images, it is the one I find
closest to the heart of total exertion.

13
00:00:50,219 --> 00:00:53,889
Because it shows most plainly
the point Part 6 began with.

14
00:00:53,969 --> 00:00:56,172
Ash is just ash, completely.

15
00:00:56,252 --> 00:01:01,407
And ash is the wood, the fire, the
air and the time, appearing as ash.

16
00:01:02,212 --> 00:01:04,582
First, firewood and ash.

17
00:01:05,384 --> 00:01:10,172
In the Genjōkōan, right after the
boat and the shore, comes firewood.

18
00:01:10,252 --> 00:01:14,566
Firewood becomes ash, and ash
cannot turn back into firewood.

19
00:01:15,372 --> 00:01:19,028
But, Dōgen says, do not
think of ash as after,

20
00:01:19,108 --> 00:01:20,731
and firewood as before.

21
00:01:20,811 --> 00:01:24,481
Firewood stays in its own
dharma position, as firewood.

22
00:01:24,561 --> 00:01:26,669
It has a before and an after.

23
00:01:26,749 --> 00:01:29,209
Yet the before and after are cut off.

24
00:01:29,289 --> 00:01:32,054
Ash stays in its own position, as ash.

25
00:01:32,848 --> 00:01:35,666
In the same way, life
does not become death.

26
00:01:35,746 --> 00:01:38,683
So we speak of no arising, and no ceasing.

27
00:01:38,763 --> 00:01:41,115
Life is a position of its own time.

28
00:01:41,195 --> 00:01:43,819
And death is a position of its own time.

29
00:01:43,899 --> 00:01:45,585
Like winter and spring.

30
00:01:45,665 --> 00:01:48,320
We do not say that winter becomes spring,

31
00:01:48,423 --> 00:01:50,331
or that spring becomes summer.

32
00:01:51,140 --> 00:01:52,844
How is this usually read?

33
00:01:52,924 --> 00:01:55,181
Each moment is complete in itself.

34
00:01:55,261 --> 00:01:58,496
And practice is not a
means to a later awakening.

35
00:01:58,576 --> 00:02:02,781
Practice and awakening are one,
each complete in its own moment.

36
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:04,978
Here is how I see it.

37
00:02:06,172 --> 00:02:08,393
The habit runs a line through time.

38
00:02:08,473 --> 00:02:10,132
Firewood is the cause.

39
00:02:10,212 --> 00:02:11,776
Ash is the effect.

40
00:02:11,856 --> 00:02:15,082
And something travels along the
line, from one to the other.

41
00:02:15,896 --> 00:02:17,682
Anatta loosens the line.

42
00:02:17,762 --> 00:02:22,238
There is no one standing on it, measuring
the moments and stitching them together.

43
00:02:23,052 --> 00:02:25,096
But here is what I most want to say.

44
00:02:25,176 --> 00:02:27,379
Ash is just ash, completely.

45
00:02:27,459 --> 00:02:29,104
It is not half firewood.

46
00:02:29,184 --> 00:02:31,604
And it is not waiting to
become something else.

47
00:02:31,684 --> 00:02:33,973
In the ash, just the ash.

48
00:02:34,768 --> 00:02:38,098
And at the same time, ash
is the wood, the fire,

49
00:02:38,178 --> 00:02:41,010
the air and the time, appearing as ash.

50
00:02:41,090 --> 00:02:44,570
Both hold, in the same
moment. Ash is complete.

51
00:02:44,650 --> 00:02:46,703
And ash is dependently arisen.

52
00:02:46,783 --> 00:02:48,972
Wood is wood, and ash is ash.

53
00:02:49,052 --> 00:02:52,224
And that is exactly how
dependent arising is met.

54
00:02:53,036 --> 00:02:55,379
Nāgārjuna says this precisely.

55
00:02:55,459 --> 00:02:59,115
Whatever arises in dependence on
something is neither that thing,

56
00:02:59,195 --> 00:03:03,495
nor different from it. And so it
is neither cut off, nor permanent.

57
00:03:04,296 --> 00:03:08,025
Dōgen's two phrases fit
Nāgārjuna's two denials.

58
00:03:08,105 --> 00:03:10,049
Cut off from before and after.

59
00:03:10,129 --> 00:03:13,052
Nothing permanent travels
from the wood into the ash.

60
00:03:13,132 --> 00:03:15,131
With a before and an after.

61
00:03:15,211 --> 00:03:18,872
The ash really does come from
the wood. It is not cut off.

62
00:03:19,684 --> 00:03:24,690
And firewood and ash are not labels on one
continuous stuff that runs through both.

63
00:03:24,770 --> 00:03:27,475
If they were, that stuff would carry over.

64
00:03:27,555 --> 00:03:31,157
And the dharma positions would
be only names on its surface.

65
00:03:31,237 --> 00:03:34,568
Nor are the positions little
moments with natures of their own.

66
00:03:34,648 --> 00:03:36,701
Part 2 found no such moment.

67
00:03:36,781 --> 00:03:38,983
They are designated, not found.

68
00:03:39,063 --> 00:03:42,588
There is no real timeline
underneath, on which they sit.

69
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:46,843
That is the taste of this
image. Nothing carried over.

70
00:03:46,923 --> 00:03:49,629
Each moment is fresh, wholly itself.

71
00:03:49,709 --> 00:03:54,035
And light, because nothing has to be
held from one moment into the next.

72
00:03:54,828 --> 00:03:59,155
And where I part. Some readers
stop at, each moment complete.

73
00:03:59,235 --> 00:04:01,288
And then the moments become islands.

74
00:04:01,368 --> 00:04:03,679
I keep the other half in the same breath.

75
00:04:03,759 --> 00:04:06,116
Complete, as its dependence.

76
00:04:06,928 --> 00:04:08,945
Second, being time.

77
00:04:09,764 --> 00:04:15,218
In another essay, Uji, Dōgen starts from
a common phrase. For the time being.

78
00:04:15,298 --> 00:04:17,120
And he reads it as being time.

79
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:20,494
Being is already time,
and time is already being.

80
00:04:21,288 --> 00:04:24,215
For the time being, standing
on the highest peak.

81
00:04:24,295 --> 00:04:28,875
For the time being, walking the
bottom of the ocean. The pine is time.

82
00:04:28,955 --> 00:04:30,533
The bamboo is time.

83
00:04:30,613 --> 00:04:34,038
Do not think, he says,
that time only flies away.

84
00:04:34,118 --> 00:04:37,286
And he says, in a line
many readers dwell on,

85
00:04:37,366 --> 00:04:40,796
that each moment is all
being, the entire world.

86
00:04:41,596 --> 00:04:43,925
He also speaks of time's passage.

87
00:04:44,005 --> 00:04:46,887
And passage, he says, is like spring.

88
00:04:46,967 --> 00:04:50,583
Spring has many features,
and that is called passage.

89
00:04:50,663 --> 00:04:53,803
But there is nothing outside
spring that passes through it.

90
00:04:53,883 --> 00:04:56,905
The passage of spring
is just spring, passing.

91
00:04:57,704 --> 00:04:59,408
How is this usually read?

92
00:04:59,488 --> 00:05:02,003
Time is not a container
that things sit in.

93
00:05:02,083 --> 00:05:04,327
And things are not apart from their time.

94
00:05:04,407 --> 00:05:07,615
Each moment is complete,
and contains all time.

95
00:05:07,695 --> 00:05:09,734
Some readers take that broadly.

96
00:05:09,814 --> 00:05:12,687
The whole universe,
present in every moment.

97
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:14,878
Here is how I see it.

98
00:05:16,072 --> 00:05:18,918
The habit makes time a river, or a road.

99
00:05:18,998 --> 00:05:21,920
Something already there,
that things move along.

100
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,189
Then time itself becomes the given.

101
00:05:24,269 --> 00:05:26,273
The floor under every event.

102
00:05:27,068 --> 00:05:28,664
Dōgen takes that away.

103
00:05:28,744 --> 00:05:33,202
There is no time apart from the pine,
the bamboo, the standing on the peak.

104
00:05:33,282 --> 00:05:37,196
Nāgārjuna examined time the
same way, and did not find it.

105
00:05:37,276 --> 00:05:39,533
Time is not found apart from things.

106
00:05:39,613 --> 00:05:43,668
And since things are not found standing
on their own, how could time be?

107
00:05:44,472 --> 00:05:47,997
The passage of spring is
the line I find most exact.

108
00:05:48,077 --> 00:05:52,046
Nothing outside spring passes
through it. There is no carrier.

109
00:05:52,126 --> 00:05:54,682
No substance of time flowing underneath.

110
00:05:54,762 --> 00:05:57,345
No thread, on which the days are strung.

111
00:05:57,425 --> 00:05:59,655
Spring passes, as spring.

112
00:05:59,734 --> 00:06:02,902
That is the dharma position,
seen from the inside.

113
00:06:02,982 --> 00:06:06,697
Passage, without anything
carried, and without a line.

114
00:06:07,492 --> 00:06:12,022
And where I part. I read each moment
is all being as total exertion.

115
00:06:12,102 --> 00:06:15,066
Nothing outside the moment
is needed to complete it.

116
00:06:15,146 --> 00:06:19,006
Nothing stands beyond it, as a
larger time in which it sits.

117
00:06:19,086 --> 00:06:22,380
I do not read it as a cosmos
packed inside the moment,

118
00:06:22,564 --> 00:06:24,631
or all of time stored in each instant.

119
00:06:24,711 --> 00:06:28,136
The moment lacks nothing because
nothing outside it is needed.

120
00:06:28,216 --> 00:06:30,369
Not because everything is stored in it.

121
00:06:31,184 --> 00:06:33,350
Third, birth and death.

122
00:06:34,164 --> 00:06:37,454
In a short essay, Shōji, Birth and Death,

123
00:06:37,534 --> 00:06:41,982
Dōgen says: just understand that
birth and death is itself nirvana.

124
00:06:42,062 --> 00:06:45,053
Then there is nothing to
escape as birth and death.

125
00:06:45,133 --> 00:06:47,068
And nothing to seek as nirvana.

126
00:06:47,872 --> 00:06:52,049
It is a mistake, he says, to
think that birth turns into death.

127
00:06:52,129 --> 00:06:56,315
Birth is a position of its own
time, with its before and after.

128
00:06:56,395 --> 00:06:58,938
When birth comes, there is just birth.

129
00:06:59,018 --> 00:07:02,633
When death comes, there is just
death. Do not cling to them.

130
00:07:02,713 --> 00:07:04,440
And do not push them away.

131
00:07:04,520 --> 00:07:08,303
This birth and death, he says,
is the life of the Buddha.

132
00:07:09,116 --> 00:07:10,820
How is this usually read?

133
00:07:10,900 --> 00:07:14,788
Freedom is not an escape from birth
and death into some other realm.

134
00:07:14,868 --> 00:07:17,505
It is meeting each fully, as it comes.

135
00:07:17,585 --> 00:07:20,915
And the life of the Buddha is
often heard as one great life,

136
00:07:20,995 --> 00:07:22,659
present in all lives.

137
00:07:23,472 --> 00:07:24,850
Here is how I see it.

138
00:07:26,044 --> 00:07:29,705
This is where firewood and ash
stop being a teaching about wood.

139
00:07:29,785 --> 00:07:34,107
When life is present, it is
completely alive. Not a prelude.

140
00:07:34,187 --> 00:07:37,599
Not a journey already heading
for a destination called death.

141
00:07:37,679 --> 00:07:40,207
When death comes, it is its own position.

142
00:07:40,287 --> 00:07:42,667
Not life, turned into something else.

143
00:07:42,747 --> 00:07:45,846
Nothing migrates across a
line from one to the other.

144
00:07:45,926 --> 00:07:48,346
Life is the total working of life.

145
00:07:48,426 --> 00:07:51,014
Death is the total working of death.

146
00:07:51,824 --> 00:07:54,258
And because each is
complete as its conditions,

147
00:07:54,338 --> 00:07:55,947
neither is sealed off.

148
00:07:56,027 --> 00:07:59,778
Life arises dependently,
wholly. And so does death.

149
00:07:59,858 --> 00:08:04,085
There is nothing to escape, because
there is no line to escape along.

150
00:08:04,165 --> 00:08:05,910
And no one standing on it.

151
00:08:06,708 --> 00:08:07,977
And where I part.

152
00:08:08,057 --> 00:08:12,135
I do not take the life of the Buddha
as one great life behind all lives,

153
00:08:12,215 --> 00:08:15,926
living through them. That would
put a Whole behind birth and death.

154
00:08:16,006 --> 00:08:19,200
Birth is complete as
birth. Death as death.

155
00:08:19,280 --> 00:08:21,270
And nothing stands behind them.

156
00:08:22,072 --> 00:08:23,668
So, which holds?

157
00:08:24,644 --> 00:08:28,346
By experience. Is ash met
as half finished firewood?

158
00:08:28,426 --> 00:08:30,125
Or as a stage on a line?

159
00:08:30,205 --> 00:08:34,759
Is this moment met as a point on a
road that runs off behind, and ahead?

160
00:08:34,839 --> 00:08:40,329
What is met is this, wholly itself,
and fresh. The ash. The spring.

161
00:08:40,409 --> 00:08:45,361
This breath. And at no point is a
thread of time met, running underneath.

162
00:08:46,176 --> 00:08:50,706
By logic. If the ash were the same as
the wood, nothing would have burned.

163
00:08:50,786 --> 00:08:54,878
If it were wholly other than the wood,
it could appear without any fire.

164
00:08:54,958 --> 00:08:57,269
If something carried
over from wood to ash,

165
00:08:57,349 --> 00:08:59,918
it would be permanent,
and could never burn.

166
00:08:59,998 --> 00:09:03,329
If nothing came from the wood,
the ash would be uncaused.

167
00:09:03,409 --> 00:09:05,706
Neither the same, nor different.

168
00:09:05,786 --> 00:09:08,224
Neither permanent, nor cut off.

169
00:09:09,028 --> 00:09:11,982
By working. Nothing
works the less for this.

170
00:09:12,062 --> 00:09:15,664
The fire still burns, and the
ash still feeds the field.

171
00:09:15,744 --> 00:09:17,730
Spring still comes after winter.

172
00:09:17,810 --> 00:09:21,688
Memory, plans and promises
work, each in its own moment.

173
00:09:22,496 --> 00:09:26,125
So the view of a line of time,
with something carried along it,

174
00:09:26,205 --> 00:09:27,556
is not the truer one.

175
00:09:27,636 --> 00:09:33,335
It is the default, carried by habit, and
by the words becomes, and turns into.

176
00:09:34,140 --> 00:09:38,331
None of this makes my everyday
words false. The wood burns to ash.

177
00:09:38,411 --> 00:09:40,056
Winter gives way to spring.

178
00:09:40,136 --> 00:09:43,765
What fails is only a
time, a wood, or a self,

179
00:09:43,845 --> 00:09:46,813
standing on its own,
and travelling across.

180
00:09:47,608 --> 00:09:51,649
This is Section 2, Part 8 of
the Dependent Arising series.

181
00:09:52,460 --> 00:09:55,564
In Part 9, I take Dōgen's image of place.

182
00:09:55,644 --> 00:09:59,807
The fish that never reaches the end
of the water, and never needs to.

